# DPoP replay-fence gap — a nonce spend rolls back with the action transaction

> **This notebook demonstrates a current LIMITATION, not a feature.** It lives under `livebooks/`
> (not packaged `documentation/`) because it shows a gap that will be closed by the independent-
> commit replay-fence API. When that fence ships, this notebook should be deleted or rewritten.
> The durable proof is the failing regression test that flips to passing when the fence lands.

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ash_onetime, "~> 0.1"},
    {:ash_postgres, "~> 2.11"},
    {:kino, "~> 0.14", only: :dev}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

## The requirement (RFC 9449 DPoP)

A DPoP proof carries a `jti` that must be unique per request. RFC 9449 §§4.2–4.3 require the
server to retain the `jti` during the acceptance window and reject reuse. Critically, **once a
valid proof has been observed, a downstream failure (e.g. token minting) must NOT make that
proof reusable** — otherwise a client that retries after a transient mint failure spends the
same proof twice.

This notebook shows that `ash_onetime`'s current `:one_time_nonce` strategy does NOT meet that
requirement, because the nonce claim commits **inside the protected action's transaction**. A
failure in the action body rolls the claim back, so the same proof is re-admitted on retry.

## Connect to PostgreSQL

```elixir
database_url =
  System.get_env(
    "DATABASE_URL",
    "ecto://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:18841/ash_onetime_test"
  )

:ok = Application.put_env(:ash, :disable_async?, true)
:ok = Application.put_env(:ash_onetime_demo, AshOnetimeDemo.Repo, url: database_url, pool_size: 10)
```

## Define the Repo, verifier, and a nonce action whose body fails

The `:mint` action is protected by `:one_time_nonce` (the DPoP `jti` is the proof). Its body
always fails — simulating a downstream token-mint failure AFTER the proof is spent.

```elixir
defmodule AshOnetimeDemo.Repo do
  use AshPostgres.Repo, otp_app: :ash_onetime_demo

  @impl AshPostgres.Repo
  def installed_extensions, do: ["ash-functions"]

  @impl AshPostgres.Repo
  def min_pg_version, do: %Version{major: 18, minor: 0, patch: 0}
end

{:ok, _} = AshOnetimeDemo.Repo.start_link()
```

```elixir
defmodule AshOnetimeDemo.ProofVerifier do
  # Stands in for DPoP jti validation: trusts the proof string as the verified key.
  def verify(proof, _context) when is_binary(proof) do
    {:ok, %AshOnetime.Verified{key: proof, issued_at: DateTime.utc_now(), verifier_id: "dpop"}}
  end

  def algorithm, do: :ed25519
  def trust_model, do: :separated
end

# The action body always fails — simulates a downstream mint failure.
defmodule AshOnetimeDemo.FailingMint do
  use Ash.Resource.Actions.Implementation

  @impl true
  def run(_input, _opts, _context),
    do: {:error, AshOnetime.Error.new(:downstream_failed, "the token mint failed")}
end

defmodule AshOnetimeDemo.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain, validate_config_inclusion?: false

  resources do
    resource AshOnetimeDemo.Token
  end
end

defmodule AshOnetimeDemo.Token do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: AshOnetimeDemo.Domain,
    data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshOnetime.Resource]

  postgres do
    table "demo_tokens"
    repo AshOnetimeDemo.Repo
  end

  multitenancy do
    strategy :context
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    action :mint, :integer do
      transaction? true
      argument :value, :integer, allow_nil?: false
      argument :proof, :string, allow_nil?: false
      run {AshOnetimeDemo.FailingMint, []}
    end
  end

  onetime do
    protect :mint do
      strategy :one_time_nonce
      scope([{:static, "dpop"}])
      key({:verified, :proof, AshOnetimeDemo.ProofVerifier})
      window(max_age: {1, :hour}, clock_skew: {5, :second})
    end
  end
end

:ok
```

## Install the admission store

```elixir
alias AshOnetimeDemo.Repo
alias Ecto.Adapters.SQL

unique = System.unique_integer([:positive])
schema = "ash_onetime_demo_#{unique}"
temp = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "ash_onetime_demo_#{unique}")
File.mkdir_p!(temp)

source =
  Mix.Tasks.AshOnetime.Gen.Migrations.render(Repo,
    hash_partitions: nil,
    partition_start: Date.utc_today() |> Date.beginning_of_month()
  )

timestamp = "20260101000000"
path = Path.join(temp, "#{timestamp}_install_ash_onetime.exs")
File.write!(path, source)
[{module, _}] = Code.compile_file(path)
version = String.to_integer(timestamp)

SQL.query!(Repo, ~s{CREATE SCHEMA "#{schema}"}, [])
:ok = Ecto.Migrator.up(Repo, version, module, prefix: schema, log: false)

SQL.query!(Repo, ~s{CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS "#{schema}"."demo_tokens" (id uuid PRIMARY KEY)}, [])
{schema, version, module}
```

## First attempt: the proof is spent, then the mint fails

The proof is admitted in `before_action` (the nonce claim commits), then the action body fails.
The whole transaction — **including the nonce claim** — rolls back.

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```elixir
{:error, error} =
  AshOnetimeDemo.Token
  |> Ash.ActionInput.for_action(:mint, %{value: 1, proof: "dpop-proof-1"})
  |> Ash.ActionInput.set_tenant(schema)
  |> Ash.run_action()

AshOnetime.Error.code(error)
```

```
:downstream_failed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```elixir
%{rows: [[nonce_claims_after_fail]]} =
  SQL.query!(Repo, ~s{SELECT count(*) FROM "#{schema}"."ash_onetime_nonce_claims"}, [])

nonce_claims_after_fail
```

```
0
```

**`0`** — the nonce claim rolled back with the action transaction. The proof's spend did not
survive the body failure.

## Second attempt: the SAME proof is re-admitted

Because the spend rolled back, the same proof is admitted again — the action body runs a second
time (and fails again). Under RFC 9449, this proof should have been **rejected** as reused.

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```elixir
second =
  AshOnetimeDemo.Token
  |> Ash.ActionInput.for_action(:mint, %{value: 1, proof: "dpop-proof-1"})
  |> Ash.ActionInput.set_tenant(schema)
  |> Ash.run_action()

second
```

```
{:error, %AshOnetime.Error{code: :downstream_failed, message: "the token mint failed", ...}}
```

The second attempt ran the body again (it returned `:downstream_failed`, not `:nonce_already_used`).
The proof was reusable — the gap.

## Clean up

```elixir
:ok = Ecto.Migrator.down(Repo, version, module, prefix: schema, log: false)
SQL.query!(Repo, ~s{DROP SCHEMA IF EXISTS "#{schema}" CASCADE}, [])
File.rm_rf!(temp)
:ok
```

## Why this happens, and the fix

The nonce claim commits via `before_action` on the protected action's transaction connection
(`lib/ash_onetime/change.ex` → `lib/ash_onetime/admission.ex` → `lib/ash_onetime/store/postgres.ex`,
no inner transaction). So any failure after `before_action` — the action body, `after_action`
hooks, a downstream mint — rolls the claim back.

The fix is an admission mode whose claim commits **independently** of the action transaction
(a `replay_fence` strategy or a `commit: :independent` option on `:one_time_nonce`), so the
proof's spend survives action rollback. That mode deliberately breaks the "claim commits with
the effect" invariant the rest of the library relies on for idempotency, so it needs its own
T2 design note (see the handoff). This notebook and its regression test are the proof that gap
is real and the fence is needed.
